+
+For interface work:
+
+- Name the requirement IDs the change addresses.
+- Preserve useful content, actual user-entered answers/notes, adaptive branches, factual safeguards, and catalog behavior.
+- Use one prominent Continue action per guided page; keep answer choices, Back, edit, and help explicit and text-labeled.
+- Do not preselect new answers, require age-65 date arithmetic, introduce hidden gestures, or add decorative animation.
+- Keep the main task and Continue near the top. Pair a compact visual lesson with the question and keep deeper explanations below, with generous breathing room.
+- Put Ask Daylight below Continue and Back in the task area, with a corresponding entry below arrival actions. Do not duplicate it in the header or lesson heading. Named picture pieces open local explanations within the picture; an explicit Ask more action can open chat.
+- Ask the four-choice goal first, then who it is for before personal coverage questions. The beginner learning route begins with what Medicare is. Grow the visual picture one relevant concept at a time; do not expose the complete A/B/D/Medigap/Advantage diagram to a newcomer. Explain why each personal question matters. Preserve an explicit experienced shortcut.
+- Inspect the rendered result on desktop, tablet, and phone. Check content preservation and actual behavior as well as relevant automated tests. Do not equate a test count with owner approval.
+- Update the requirements change log when the owner changes a requirement. Report remaining gaps honestly; do not mark a requirement implemented on intent alone.
+
+Existing `DAYLIGHT_*PLAN.md` and design briefs contain historical and conflicting proposals. Follow the precedence recorded in `PRODUCT.md`; they do not override the owner's latest requirements.
+
+The workspace contains substantial uncommitted work. Use targeted patches; do not reset the repository or discard unrelated changes. Preserve backend review/acceptance gates, source evidence, and accurate privacy behavior.
