+## RC19 · Specific Special Needs Plan eligibility · internal check/missing-details-only use approved
+
+**Necessity assessment first:** Suppress the generic consumer proof question/menu. Keep a plan/type-specific eligibility verification check, reusing the program, A/B and location facts already known. Explain a potentially relevant plan type in plain language when volunteered facts or the user's request make it useful; do not require a user to know to ask for an SNP before self-discovery is possible. Establish the actual candidate/type/year and its published requirements before collecting further sensitive eligibility facts. No broad diagnosis, institutional-care or financial interview follows from Medicaid, a medicine/provider list or disability. Current inventory scope is flags/referral; detailed matching remains future/held, so collecting data that cannot advance supported work is inappropriate. Missing plan verification is usually a system/plan next step, not another personal question.
+
+**Original inventory question:** For the Special Needs Plan type you want to explore, what exact qualifying program or plan confirmation do you have?
+
+**Original options:** Program or plan confirmation · No confirmation · Do not explore this type.
+
+**Critique:** The original expects the user to understand both Special Needs Plan and what evidence qualifies. Its options conflate evidence availability with a preference not to explore and omit unknown. The two meanings must remain separate. No confirmation/unknown never means ineligible; a preference not to explore is not an eligibility decision. A broad program report can justify further consideration without proving a specific plan accepts that person's exact category.
+
+**Common zero-ask flow (illustrative, not actual customer facts):** T05/RC18/chat already reports the person's Medicare/Medicaid program; earlier answers supply location and target year. The person asks Can I join [named plan]? Daylight checks the published requirements for that exact plan/type/year against sufficiently scoped known facts. Reuse them; do not ask which programs the user receives again or request proof generically. Record any exact criterion/official confirmation still needed and offer verification/referral while keeping the relevant option visibly unconfirmed. If the supported task cannot advance with another user fact, ask nothing further.
+
+**A useful targeted clarification:** The person volunteers [named plan] sent me a letter last year saying I could join, and the requested coverage year is already known. If whether that letter applies to the target year is genuinely unresolved and consequential, ask only:
+
+**Proposed contextual follow-up:** Does that letter say it applies to [requested year] coverage?
+
+**Inline options:** Yes · It names a different year · I don't know · I don't have it handy. Accept free text/defer and offer help finding the relevant wording only if useful. No full upload or document hunt is required. Reuse an already clear plan/year confirmation instead of asking again. A letter-year report supplies only that scope fact; it never verifies every eligibility requirement or continued program status. For a different genuine gap, ask the named missing fact rather than using this as a stock RC19 script.
