+## RC24 · Rule-specific missing detail · Internal check approved
+
+**Refinement after the owner's why-would-this-matter challenge, October 27 (proposal):** The concrete decision is which benefit/rule evidence to investigate, not collecting an administration preference. [Medicare insulin guidance](https://www.medicare.gov/coverage/insulin), checked October 27, distinguishes insulin used with a Part-B-covered DME pump from insulin methods/pumps that may fall under Part D. A broad self-versus-professional answer does not identify the pump/type and can still miss the relevant rule fact. Therefore RC24 is best treated as an internal source-specific dependency check, with only the actual missing discriminator asked. There is no default clinic/pharmacy or who-administers question. Method/device/model/type can already be supplied through T07/RC22; reuse it. If a person reports a pump but the actual required type/model remains unclear, a relevant question is Which insulin pump do you use? with actually sourced options and typed/unknown alternatives. A pump answer alone must never default to Part B; exact DME qualification and other applicable conditions remain externally verified. Missing that fact holds the dependent benefit/price conclusion, not unrelated work. This explains a real use case without making every medicine an administration interview; no product-use approval or personal coverage acceptance follows.
+
+**Necessity assessment first:** Retain RC24 as a narrow missing-detail check inside the medicine conversation, not a routine clinic/pharmacy question or an automatic step after RC23. Ask only when an actual supported requested rule depends on a still-missing administration fact. Reuse T07/RC22 reports and RC23 dispensing facts to their actual scope. A prior-authorization/step-therapy/quantity flag alone does not make administration relevant; neither a product being injectable nor an unspecified coverage problem earns an administration interview. Missing external source/policy evidence remains an external hold. The existing universal RC23 dependency must not force a fill-price questionnaire before a narrower administration-dependent check.
+
+**Critical issue with the original:** The alternatives mix two facts: where a medicine is obtained and who/where administers it. A person can collect it at a pharmacy and have a clinician give it elsewhere, so these answers are not mutually exclusive. A health professional can administer at home or a pharmacy; home does not establish self-administration. Both and Not sure must be separate states. Clinic is not an automatic Part B conclusion and pharmacy is not an automatic Part D conclusion. Exact applicable rules and evidence still decide; reported setting only guides investigation.
+
+**Original inventory question:** Is this given at a clinic or filled at a pharmacy?
+
+**Original options:** At a clinic · At a pharmacy · Both / Not sure.
+
+**Example contextual wording, only when who administers is the actual rule-specific missing dependency; no default question:** Do you take or give yourself [medicine], or does a health professional give it to you?
+
+**Proposed unselected dropdown:** I take it or give it to myself · A health professional gives it to me · It varies · Another way · I don't know. Preserve typed clarification, correction and I'll answer later. Another way/varies can reveal relevant meaning if needed; they are not unknown by default. Do not expose medical-rule jargon or ask the person to choose Part B versus Part D.
+
+**Brief reason when useful:** How a medicine is given can affect which coverage rules we need to check.
+
+**Ask the actual missing dimension:** If who gives it is already known but a source condition still needs where, ask only Where does [the named health professional] give you [medicine]? with relevant place options, another/unknown and typed details. Do not re-ask the full who question. If both who and place are genuinely needed, group those two concise controls in the same turn rather than serialize them. Ask no exact address, diagnosis or full treatment history merely to complete this enum. These are contextual details of existing RC24, not new primary inventory IDs; G11 explicitly holds missing per-item/administration representations before runtime use.
+
+**Prior-answer flow (synthetic, not actual customer data or a live classification):** T07/RC22 identifies the medicine and RC23 identifies the dispensing pharmacy. A reviewed rule for the requested coverage issue still depends on whether the person or a health professional gives it. That earns the named-medicine question above; a known pharmacy alone cannot supply the answer. A clear response supplies the reported administration fact without an extra echo. If It varies is consequential, clarify only the relevant intended situation/period, preserving both reports. A source-specific location detail is requested only if still missing and actually necessary; no complete fill interview is required first.
+
+**Zero-ask flow:** T07 already says The nurse gives me [medicine] at home. Reuse both who and where, skip RC24 and check the actual rule/evidence. Likewise, general source-supported education or a supported narrower check needing no administration context skips it. Collecting at a pharmacy and receiving from a nurse elsewhere is not a contradiction. No source gap earns a repeat intake, and unknown/defer is valid while the dependent conclusion stays held.
+
+**Coverage-problem scope correction:** If the user reports a rejection/approval problem, ask only a specific missing problem fact when the requested supported help needs it. Do not replay the clinic/pharmacy question if setting is already known or irrelevant. The RC24 choice cannot encode a denial reason, authorization, exception status or clinical necessity. Retain original reports and G11's explicit representation/evidence gap; neither a restriction flag nor an administration selection silently supplies these facts. No generic exception/diagnosis screen is approved by this review.
